---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ToolEmbed from '../components/ToolEmbed.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="AI Sovereignties"
  description="A live map of the infrastructure behind AI in Australia, and the public debate forming around it. Data centres, mines, refineries, energy and water, and the lines of ownership running through them."
  ogImage="/images/og-sovereignties.png"
  dataset={{
    name: "Australia's Critical AI Infrastructure Tracker",
    description: "A live, openly maintained map of the infrastructure behind AI in Australia: data centres, rare-earth mines, refineries, and the energy and water they draw, with ownership, approval pathways, and the public contestation forming around each site.",
    keywords: ["AI sovereignty", "data centres", "critical minerals", "AI infrastructure", "Australia", "compute", "rare earths"],
    spatialCoverage: "Australia",
  }}
>
  <main class="container sov">

    <header class="sov-header">
      <p class="sov-eyebrow">Civic Interplay / Sovereignties</p>
      <h1>AI Sovereignties</h1>
      <p class="sov-intro">
        A live map of the infrastructure behind AI in Australia, and the public
        debate forming around it. Data centres, mines, refineries, the energy
        and water they draw, and the lines of ownership running through them.
        Each point is a real site on the ground.
      </p>
    </header>

    <ToolEmbed
      title="Mapping AI Sovereignties"
      src="https://sovereignties.civicinterplay.io/"
      openLabel="Open the map in a new tab"
      height="820px"
    />
    <p class="sov-note">
      Not loading? Your browser may be blocking the embedded site. Use the <a href="https://sovereignties.civicinterplay.io/" target="_blank" rel="noopener external">open in a new tab</a> link to launch it directly.
    </p>

    <section class="sov-section">
      <h2>What you're looking at</h2>
      <p>
        This is a living atlast of an increasingly contested historical terrain. 
        The primary focus is on critical infrastructure required for the national AI sovereignty agenda to be realised on Australian soil. 
        This focus includes data centres, mines, refineries, and related
        energy or water infrastructure. 
        
        The legend does lots of work. Different ownership and governance models are called out - not just who owns but also who approves, and who contests. 
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="sov-section">
      <h2>Whose voices are heard</h2>
      <p>
        The Australian debates are following closely behind widespread public disapproval of data centre construction, particularly in areas facing water stress or growing energy prices. 
        The tracker is listening to who is contesting the infrastructure roll-out, and those who advocate for it. 
        The opposition to data centre approvals is taking place at a time when many government agencies are onboarding AI enablement officers, and the AI capabilities are becoming essential in the workforce. 
        Australia has historically been fast-adopters of new technology, and this wave of agentic disruption is no different. Many Australians are enjoying the MCP frisson, even if they begrudge the data centre expansion. 
      </p>
      <p>
        This is intentionally being crafted out in the open. A focus is on approval rates, the practices of resistance being deployed, and the roles-played by different government agencies, whether local government, State Government agencies, or Federal Government. 
        The different patterns for AI stack building are of great interest. A hyperscale data centre in a territory facing water shortage demonstrates a high degree of allegiance to hyperscale-subject relations, at the expense of other forms of civic belonging. 
        Investment in a mix of local compute, AI innovation ecosystems and critical-curious minds, combined with reflexive self-awareness of the dangers of deepening dependencies, underpinned by supportive agencies and alternate infrastructures and 'AI enablement' is the preferred pathway. 
        All eyes and ears are open for this pattern in the landscape. 
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="https://studio-esem.notion.site/Contestation-sovereignty-trackers-full-session-38d18df0b77581f697dfe059a69b454d" target="_blank" rel="noopener external">See the contestation record</a>, the trackers behind the map, in Notion.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="sov-section">
      <h2>The rise and rise of a term</h2>
      <p>
        The term "AI sovereignty" went from near-absent to everywhere-all-at-once in a short time frame. 
        The chart below captures the change. Very low in 2022, a spike in early 2025 (Nvidia, Davos) and a peak continuing into 2026.
      </p>
      <img
        class="sov-chart"
        src="/images/ai-sovereignty-timeline.svg"
        alt="Line chart of global news coverage mentioning 'sovereign AI' or 'AI sovereignty', rising from near-zero in 2022 to a peak in 2026."
        loading="lazy"
      />
      <p class="sov-chart-credit">
        Source: GDELT news-coverage volume (relative),
        <a href="https://app.notion.com/p/38e18df0b77581f5a06cd1b03bfd4707" target="_blank" rel="noopener external">data and method</a>.
        More in the
        <a href="https://studio-esem.notion.site/AI-Sovereignties-project-explainer-38d18df0b77581ab8465dcb65a6628ad" target="_blank" rel="noopener external">project explainer</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="sov-section">
      <h2>Where the data comes from</h2>
      <p>
        The map sources data from a Critical Infrastructure Tracker, a database built in Notion updated every two weeks through a mix of agent-based research and human-in-the-loop weaving. The data is sourced agentically, supported through Claude Code, with compute costs also tracked recursively. The contestation record is built from
        planning submissions, local and national press, local governments and other inquiry
        material. 
        Corrections are welcome; the effort feels (for now) gestural rather than comprehensive. 
      </p>
      <p>
        Spot a missing site, an error, or contestation we have not captured? <a href="https://studio-esem.notion.site/a504252cf109454598a7b02e5024b10d" target="_blank" rel="noopener external">Submit it here</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="sov-section">
      <h2>Methods, citation and licence</h2>
      <p>
        Formally, this is <em>a living atlas of contesting and curating AI sovereignties (Australian view)</em>. How each entry is sourced and classified is set out in the <a href="https://studio-esem.notion.site/AI-Sovereignties-project-explainer-38d18df0b77581ab8465dcb65a6628ad" target="_blank" rel="noopener external">project explainer</a>. Entries are coded by agent and flagged for human review (agent, human, or human-verified), so the basis of each classification stays visible.
      </p>
      <p>
        The project's methodology, classification and written analysis are openly licensed under <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener external">CC BY 4.0</a>. The underlying records are compiled from public sources, cited per entry; the facts themselves carry no such claim. Cite as: Sarah Barns, <em>A living atlas of contesting and curating AI sovereignties (Australian view)</em>, Civic Interplay, 2026. <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21026430" target="_blank" rel="noopener external">doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21026430</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <aside class="sov-cost">
      <p class="sov-cost-label">The cost of this research</p>
      <p>
        This project is built as an agentic, recursively-attuned practice of civic AI. 
        Across five working sessions in partnership with Claude Code, between April and June 2026, it has used <em>roughly</em> the
        energy of <strong>12 phone charges</strong>, <strong>one and a half glasses</strong>
        of water, and the carbon of a <strong>350 m car trip</strong> (about 156 Wh, 358 mL
        and 64 g CO₂). The compute costs are self-reported by Claude Code, and probably very unreliable, worthy of being disputed.
        They are logged openly in the
        <a href="https://studio-esem.notion.site/10618c7776a749fb8051ee4e8d1df570?v=4b15487e90c245358d7cae4d7c85cd89" target="_blank" rel="noopener external">Cost of Compute tracker</a>.
        Rough estimates; snapshot 29 June 2026.
      </p>
    </aside>

    <footer class="sov-footer">
      <p>
        Find this map alongside the other tools on the <a href="/doing">Do page</a>.
        Questions or corrections? <a href="https://sarahbarns.com" rel="external">Reach out</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Presented at the American Association of Geographers Annual Conference, San Francisco, April 2026. <a href="/aag-barns-2026.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View the slides (PDF)</a>.
      </p>
    </footer>

  </main>

  <style>
    .sov {
      padding: 3rem 2rem 4rem;
      max-width: 880px;
    }

    .sov-header {
      margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
      padding-bottom: 2rem;
      border-bottom: 2px solid var(--border);
    }

    .sov-eyebrow {
      font-family: var(--heading);
      font-size: 0.7rem;
      font-weight: 500;
      letter-spacing: 0.12em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      color: var(--text-faint);
      margin-bottom: 0.6rem;
    }

    .sov-header h1 {
      font-family: var(--heading);
      font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3rem);
      font-weight: 300;
      letter-spacing: 0.04em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      margin-bottom: 0.6rem;
    }

    .sov-intro {
      font-family: var(--body);
      font-size: 0.95rem;
      font-style: italic;
      color: var(--text-dim);
      max-width: 62ch;
    }

    .sov-note {
      font-family: var(--heading);
      font-size: 0.78rem;
      color: var(--text-faint);
      margin-top: 1rem;
      max-width: 65ch;
    }

    .sov-note a {
      color: var(--text-dim);
      text-decoration: underline;
      text-underline-offset: 3px;
    }

    .sov-note a:hover {
      color: var(--purple);
    }

    .sov-section {
      margin-top: 3rem;
    }

    .sov-section h2 {
      font-family: var(--heading);
      font-size: clamp(1.4rem, 3vw, 2rem);
      font-weight: 400;
      letter-spacing: 0.04em;
      margin-bottom: 0.8rem;
    }

    .sov-section p {
      font-family: var(--body);
      font-size: 1rem;
      line-height: 1.7;
      color: var(--text);
      max-width: 65ch;
      margin-bottom: 1rem;
    }

    .sov-section p a {
      color: var(--purple);
      text-decoration: underline;
      text-underline-offset: 3px;
    }

    .sov-footer {
      margin-top: 3.5rem;
      padding-top: 2rem;
      border-top: 2px solid var(--border);
    }

    .sov-footer p {
      font-family: var(--body);
      font-size: 0.95rem;
      color: var(--text-dim);
      max-width: 60ch;
    }

    .sov-footer a {
      color: var(--purple);
      text-decoration: underline;
      text-underline-offset: 3px;
    }

    .sov-chart {
      display: block;
      width: 100%;
      height: auto;
      margin-top: 1.25rem;
      border: 2px solid var(--border);
      border-radius: var(--radius);
      box-shadow: 4px 4px 0 var(--border);
      background: #fafafa;
    }

    .sov-section p.sov-chart-credit {
      font-family: var(--heading);
      font-size: 0.78rem;
      color: var(--text-faint);
      margin-top: 0.8rem;
    }

    .sov-section p.sov-chart-credit a {
      color: var(--purple);
      text-decoration: underline;
      text-underline-offset: 3px;
    }

    .sov-cost {
      margin-top: 3rem;
      border: 2px solid var(--border);
      border-left: 4px solid #D16D54;
      background: #faf6f0;
      border-radius: var(--radius);
      box-shadow: 4px 4px 0 var(--border);
      padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem;
    }

    .sov-cost-label {
      font-family: var(--heading);
      font-size: 0.7rem;
      font-weight: 500;
      letter-spacing: 0.12em;
      text-transform: uppercase;
      color: var(--text-faint);
      margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
    }

    .sov-cost p:last-child {
      font-family: var(--body);
      font-size: 0.95rem;
      line-height: 1.7;
      color: var(--text);
      max-width: 65ch;
      margin: 0;
    }

    .sov-cost a {
      color: var(--purple);
      text-decoration: underline;
      text-underline-offset: 3px;
    }

    @media (max-width: 640px) {
      .sov {
        padding: 2rem 1rem 3rem;
      }
    }
  </style>
</BaseLayout>
